/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a monostatic radar.
   - The target intercepts power equal to this density times its radar cross section σ (in m²), and reradiates it. σ is defined so that the reradiation can be treated as equal in all directions (see Radar cross section of targets).
   - The antenna collects this density over its effective aperture Ae.
   - Pr is the received echo power in W; Pt the peak transmit power in W; G the antenna gain as a ratio; λ the wavelength in m; σ the radar cross section in m²; R the range in m.
   - In terms of aperture the same result is Pr = PtAe²σ/(4πλ²R⁴).
   - The receiver adds its own noise.
   - Smin = kT0BF·(S/N)min Here Smin is the minimum detectable signal in W; B is the receiver noise bandwidth in Hz, about 1/τ for a pulse of width τ; F and (S/N)min are ratios.
   - Losses in the waveguide, duplexer, radome and signal processing are gathered into a system loss L, a ratio greater than 1, which divides the echo: the useful power is Pr/L.
   - A radar rarely decides from one pulse.
   - Set Pr/L = Smin and solve for R:
*/
