/* book: lead: A radar sees the echo a target sends back, not its outline. Two objects of the same size can return echoes ten thousand times apart, because the echo depends on shape, aspect angle and wavelength. Radar engineers describe a target by one number, its radar cross section σ: an equivalent area in square metres. It sits in the radar range equation and decides how far away a target can be seen; stealth */
/* book Figure 6.3.1: The exact cross section of a metal sphere, divided by πa². Small spheres follow 9(ka)⁴, so the raindrop of the second example is 123 times brighter at 10 GHz than at 3 GHz; near ka = 1 the curve peaks at 3.65; above ka ≈ 10 it settles at πa², where the first example's sphere (ka = 118) sits. */
