/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A target intercepts some of the incident power and scatters it in many directions.
   - σ is the radar cross section in m²; R is the target range in m; Si is the incident power density at the target and Ss the scattered power density back at the radar, both in W/m².
   - σ spans many powers of ten, so it is often given in decibels relative to 1 m²: σ(dBsm) = 10 log10(σ / 1 m²).
   - σ is not the shadow area.
   - The metal sphere is the one target whose σ is known exactly at every frequency, and it looks the same from every side.
   - A flat metal plate seen face-on is the opposite extreme.
   - A is the plate area in m²; λ is the wavelength in m.
   - A corner reflector is three metal plates at right angles (a trihedral).
   - Real targets are collections of panels, edges, cavities and engine inlets.
   - These are orders of magnitude only; the same target changes by 20 dB between nose-on and broadside.
*/
