/* book Worked example 2: Q: A raindrop is a sphere of radius 1 mm. Find its σ at 3 GHz and at 10 GHz, using the Rayleigh formula σ = 9πa²(ka)⁴, and the ratio of the two.
   1. At 3 GHz: λ = 0.1 m; ka = 2π × 0.001 / 0.1 = 0.0628 ≪ 1, so the Rayleigh formula applies.
   2. σ = 9π × (10⁻³)² × (0.0628)⁴ = 2.827 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.559 × 10⁻⁵ = 4.41 × 10⁻¹⁰ m² (−93.6 dBsm).
   3. At 10 GHz: λ = 0.03 m; ka = 0.2094; σ = 2.827 × 10⁻⁵ × 1.924 × 10⁻³ = 5.44 × 10⁻⁸ m² (−72.6 dBsm).
   4. Ratio: (10/3)⁴ = 123.5, that is 20.9 dB, exactly the 1/λ⁴ law.
   Answer: σ = 4.41 × 10⁻¹⁰ m² at 3 GHz and 5.44 × 10⁻⁸ m² at 10 GHz: the X-band radar sees each drop 123 times (20.9 dB) more strongly, which is why rain clutter is a high-band problem. */
